In article <26699@usc.edu> bayliss@skat.usc.edu (Drew Bayliss) writes:
>Fishy decided to chow down on my 
>recently dropped food rations. Normally I have no problem with
>this, but the little mutt ate ALL 10 OF MY BLOODY FOOD RATIONS!
...
>Is this a bug or a feature or did Fishy eat a cursed ring of hunger?

None of the above.  This is what happens when you implement a program
before designing it.  The 10 food rations are treated as one item for
some purposes.  Fishy eats all or none.  Fishy picks up all or none.
Isn't that easy to code up?  You don't have to code up lots of
interesting case, much less think of them.

Spoiler: Since 10 food items are quite heavy, Fishy can't possibly
pick them up.  If you want her to steal food rations for you I think
you should limit your piles to 6 (not sure).

Remember to copy those save files.  Think of it as a creative form of
civil disobedience.

